\section{Who decides}{

Resume is resolved once per app, and the first of these that applies wins:
\enumerate{
\item The app: its last \code{enableResume()} or \code{disableResume()} call.
\item The hosting platform: the \code{SHINY_RESUME} environment variable (\code{TRUE}
or \code{FALSE}). A platform that turns resume on gets \code{enableResume()}'s
defaults.
\item The default: on when running locally, off under Shiny Server or Posit
Connect.
}

With resume on, the browser retries a lost connection on any server, up
to ten times, and \code{session$allowReconnect()} has nothing to add (see
\link{session}).
}

\section{Saved state}{

Saved state is written to the hosting platform's bookmark storage when it
provides one, otherwise to \code{tools::R_user_dir("shiny", "cache")}; on POSIX
systems only the owner can read it. It is kept for 24 hours. A value that
cannot be serialized (it holds a connection, an R6 object, or a closure) is
not saved, and \code{session$resumeReport()} says so.
